\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Appendix I of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The role of demographic feedback on the evolution of developmental windows
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We model a large population of asexual individuals,
 distributed in a metapopulation across habitat patches of equal size and quality,
 but each with a population density state with transition probabilities determined by the local population's growth rate.
 Each individual has three state dimensions—
life stage (
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

),
 patch information (
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

),
 and energy reserves (
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

)—
upon which their developmental strategy is dependent,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,i,e)$
\end_inset

,
 which determines the fraction of their current energy reserves they will invest in life stage transitions in immature life stages,
 and in reproductive effort in mature life stages.
 An individual's patch information state gives them the subjective probability that the local population density of their patch is high.
 Finally,
 individuals must pay a maintenance cost depending on their overall size (
\begin_inset Formula $s+e$
\end_inset

),
 with a probability of death associated with their posterior energy reserves state.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We assume mutations are rare and of small effect such that effectively all individuals across the metapopulation have the same state-dependent developmental strategy (henceforth referred to as the resident strategy).
 Given the resident strategy we then do a forward projection using a Markov chain until demographic stability is reached at the metapopulation level (i.e.
 among patches).
 Given the probability distribution of population density states of patches,
 and assuming individuals have complete information as to this probability distribution and of the transition probabilities between patch pop.
 density states,
 we find the best response strategy for an invading individual (i.e.
 the developmental strategy that maximises their expected fitness).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Growth cycle
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We model the growth cycle of both individuals and the influence of their actions on local population densities (see figure 1).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Newborns disperse into random patches,
 with accurate prior information as to the probability distribution over patch population density states,
 and causing transitions in patch population density states.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individuals forage,
 with food availability being a random variable,
 normally distributed,
 with mean determined by the population density.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individuals update their patch information state given informativeness of food availability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Food is added to their energy reserves state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individuals choose what proportion of their energy reserves to invest in life stage development,
 or reproductive effort if they are mature,
 given their energy reserves state,
 life stage state,
 and belief state about the probability that they are in a high population density patch.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individuals die with probability given whether they can pay their maintenance costs from their reserves after making their developmental and reproductive investments.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Mass deaths can also occur from exogenous factors (e.g.
 disease outbreaks) influenced by the patch population density state,
 expressed through transitions to lower population densities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
State dimensions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Patch population density state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

,
 corresponding to low and high population density states respectively.
 We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 determines the probability of patch quality decline and determining the availability of food.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Life stage state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $s\in\{0,1,...,S-1,S\}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $S-1$
\end_inset

 is a mature scrambler,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 is a mature fighter (with reproductive benefit of 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{fighter}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Patch belief state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $i=\{0,1/I,2/I,...,(I-1)/I,I/I\}$
\end_inset

,
 is an individual's implicit and subjective probability that the population density state of their patch is high (i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $P(n=1)$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Energy reserves state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $e\in\{0,1,...,E-1,E\}$
\end_inset

,
 corresponding to the reserves of energy they have available for paying maintenance costs,
 life stage transition investments,
 and reproductive investments.
 If an individual achieves energy reserves above the threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{switch}$
\end_inset

,
 they transition into a mature fighter,
 else a scrambler.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Submodels
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Patch density states
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we describe pseudocode for calculating the demographically stable probability distribution of population densities across patches and of the transition probabilities between patch density states.
 Patches have a population size state 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

.
 The actual population size for any given state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
q_{n}=q_{min}\cdot(n+1)\label{eq:}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{min}$
\end_inset

 is the maximum population size of any patch.
 Then the probability distribution over patch states in the population is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\eta=P(q_{n}),\label{eq:-50}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which will emerge from actions of individuals using the resident developmental strategy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Physiology of newborns
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ideally,
 individuals would be born with precise and accurate prior information regarding the probability distribution over the population density states of patches.
 Individuals would then update their information state given cues for the population density of their patch which come from the availability of food,
 see section 1.2.
 Individuals are born at life stage state 0 and an energy reserves state of 1,
 and arrive at their respective patches with a prior belief state over the local population density of
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(n=1)=\tilde{\eta},\label{eq:-3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\nospellcheck off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\xout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\eta}$
\end_inset


\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\nospellcheck default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\xout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
 was the true proportion of patches that are of density state 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset

 in their evolutionary history,
 emerging from the previous resident strategy and updated with each subsequent invasion (see section 5.2).
 Therefore,
 in subsequent invasions to form a discrete grid of states that we can solve a Markov decision process for we need interpolate 
\begin_inset Formula $P(n=1)$
\end_inset

 onto a grid of possible discrete subjective probability values,
 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\{0/I,1/I,2/I,...,I/I\}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is an integer determining the fineness of the grid.
 The first step of interpolation is to find the floor of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\eta}$
\end_inset

 when it is scaled by the grid size,
 which will give us the nearest grid cell 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 below the continuous value
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
i=\lfloor\tilde{\eta}\cdot I\rfloor,\label{eq:-20}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and subsequently the difference is the fractional part 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f=\tilde{\eta}\cdot I-i.\label{eq:-21}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We can then use the fractional part as a probability that the state should be interpolated to be 
\begin_inset Formula $(i+1)$
\end_inset

,
 finally giving us two discrete states and associated probabilities for defining the state distribution of migrants:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\rho}(s=0,i,m=1)=1-f,\label{eq:-24}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\rho}(s=0,i+1,m=1)=f\label{eq:-25}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Development
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each individual,
 choose a developmental action given their state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,i,e\}$
\end_inset

,
 given the resident developmental strategy for that state 
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,i,e)\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Their developmental action determines the proportion of their energy reserves they invest in life-stage development or in reproduction (if they are mature),
 generating a transition in their life stage 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and their energy reserves 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The resulting transition probabilities,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P(s^{\prime},e^{\prime}|x,s,i,e)$
\end_inset

,
 between state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s^{\prime},i,e^{\prime}\}$
\end_inset

 and state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,i,e\}$
\end_inset

 given the resident developmental strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,i,e)$
\end_inset

 and discounted survival probabilities,
 are found be doing the following:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,i,e\}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(s^{\prime},e^{\prime}|x,s,i,e)=0.\label{eq:-10}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Else,
 they invest 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta e$
\end_inset

 reserves energy into development,
 defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta e=e\cdot x(s,i,e)\label{eq:-20-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

given their developmental strategy.
 This gives them an interpolate posterior energy state distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e_{1}=\lfloor e-\Delta e\rfloor,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e_{2}=e_{1}+1,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(e_{2})=(e-\Delta e)-e_{1},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(e_{1})=1-P(e_{2}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $s\geq S-1$
\end_inset

,
 they are of a mature life stage and so no developmental transition can occur,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $P(s^{\prime}=s,e^{\prime}=e|x,s,i,e)=1$
\end_inset

,
 their investment instead goes towards reproduction (see the next subsection).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If they are of the pre-adult life stage (
\begin_inset Formula $s=S-3$
\end_inset

),
 depending on the size of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta e$
\end_inset

 and the investment threshold above which the fighter phenotype can be expressed,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{switch}$
\end_inset

,
 they have a probability of not transitioning at all 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 and a probability of transitioning to a mature scrambler 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{scramb}$
\end_inset

,
 or transitioning to a mature fighter 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{fighter}$
\end_inset

.
 If their investment in growth scaled by their maximum possible investment,
 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{g}=\frac{\Delta e}{E}$
\end_inset

,
 is greater than or equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{switch}$
\end_inset

,
 these probabilities are defined as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{fighter}=\frac{e_{g}-f_{switch}}{1-f_{switch}},p_{scramb}=1-p_{fighter},p_{0}=0.
\]

\end_inset

else if 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{g}<f_{switch}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{fighter}=0,p_{scramb}=\frac{e_{g}}{f_{switch}},p_{0}=1-p_{scramb}.
\]

\end_inset

giving transition probabilities of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(s^{\prime}=S-2,e^{\prime}=e_{2}|x,s,i,e)=p_{0}\cdot P(e_{2}),
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(s^{\prime}=S-2,e^{\prime}=e_{1}|x,s,i,e)=p_{0}\cdot P(e_{1}),
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(s^{\prime}=S-1,e^{\prime}=e_{2}|x,s,i,e)=p_{scramb}\cdot P(e_{2}),
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(s^{\prime}=S-1,e^{\prime}=e_{1}|x,s,i,e)=p_{scramb}\cdot P(e_{1}),
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(s^{\prime}=S,e^{\prime}=e_{2}|x,s,i,e)=p_{fighter}\cdot P(e_{2}),
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(s^{\prime}=S,e^{\prime}=e_{1}|x,s,i,e)=p_{fighter}\cdot P(e_{1}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
else they have a probability distribution over posterior life stages of
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
s_{2}=s+1,P(s_{2})=\frac{\Delta e}{E},\label{eq:-19-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{1}=s,P(s_{1})=1-\frac{\Delta e}{E},
\]

\end_inset

which we can then interpolate on to the discrete grid,
 giving transition probabilities of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(s^{\prime}=s_{2},e^{\prime}=e_{2}|x,s,i,e)=P(s_{2})\cdot P(e_{2})
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(s^{\prime}=s_{2},e^{\prime}=e_{1}|x,s,i,e)=P(s_{2})\cdot P(e_{1})
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(s^{\prime}=s_{1},e^{\prime}=e_{2}|x,s,i,e)=P(s_{1})\cdot P(e_{2})
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(s^{\prime}=s_{1},e^{\prime}=e_{1}|x,s,i,e)=P(s_{1})\cdot P(e_{1})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Reproduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Adults,
 individuals of size state 
\begin_inset Formula $s=S$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $s=S-1$
\end_inset

,
 develop offspring asexually at a rate dependent on their developmental strategy,
 phenotype,
 and energy reserves.
 We here define 
\begin_inset Formula $R(s,i,e)$
\end_inset

,
 the expected reproductive rate of an individual of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,i,e\}$
\end_inset

,
 using strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 (where for the resident population 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa=x(s,i,e)$
\end_inset

) as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R(s,i,e,\kappa)=\begin{cases}
\text{if \ensuremath{s=S}} & \kappa\cdot e\cdot(r_{e}+r_{fighter})\\
\text{if \ensuremath{s=S-1}} & \kappa\cdot e\cdot(r_{e})\\
\text{else}: & 0
\end{cases}\label{eq:-1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 is the developmental strategy of the individual for its given state and the proportion of energy they will invest in development of offspring,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{e}$
\end_inset

 is the baseline fecundity per unit of energy invested,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{fighter}$
\end_inset

 is reproductive benefit of expressing the fighter phenotype expressed if they are in the alternative mature life stage 
\begin_inset Formula $s=S$
\end_inset

,
 allowing them to better extract auxiliary resources for reproduction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Foraging
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals observe cues for population density in the form of food availability.
 Food availability is a random variable influenced by the population density,
 all patches produce the same amount of food which must be shared,
 so for any given patch of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 individuals receive a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g\sim\text{Normal}(\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{n}},\sigma_{g})\label{eq:-14}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{g}$
\end_inset

 is the mean food mass production of any patch and parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{g}$
\end_inset

 is the food mass standard deviation.
 However,
 as we are working with discrete mathematics,
 we must approximate this normal distribution using a pseudo-normal probability distribution over a grid of possible food mass values 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{G}=\{0,1,...,G_{max}\}$
\end_inset

,
 with likelihoods for each realised food mass being 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ccc}
L(g|q_{n})=\frac{1}{K}e^{-\frac{(g-\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{n}})^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}_{g}}} & \text{for} & g\in\boldsymbol{G},\end{array}\label{eq:-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is a suitable integer that determines the fineness of the grid of values,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
K=\underset{-G}{\overset{G}{\boldsymbol{\sum}}}e^{-\frac{(g-\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{n}})^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}_{g}}}.\label{eq:-15}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Observation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the food mass 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 individuals observe the resulting nutrient data and update their population density information states (i.e.
 their belief states).
 Assuming Bayesian optimal updating,
 their posterior belief is defined 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall i$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(n=1|g)=\frac{(i/I)\cdot L(g|q_{1})}{(i/I)\cdot L(g|q_{1})+(1-(i/I))\cdot L(g|q_{0})}.\label{eq:-16}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Where 
\begin_inset Formula $(i/I)$
\end_inset

 is their prior.
 The posterior belief state 
\begin_inset Formula $P(n=1|g)$
\end_inset

 (the probability that the local density is n=1 given food mass cue 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

) then needs to be interpolated onto a discrete grid of possible values of 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 To this end,
 the first step is to decompose each 
\begin_inset Formula $P(n=1|g)$
\end_inset

 into it's integer and fractional parts,
 with the integer part defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
i^{\prime}=\lfloor P(n=1|g)\cdot I\rfloor,\label{eq:-8}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and subsequently the fractional part defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f=P(n=1|g)\cdot I-i^{\prime}.\label{eq:-9}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This then gives the transition probabilities between discrete belief states
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(i^{\prime}|i,g)=1-f,\label{eq:-7}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(i^{\prime}+1|i,g)=f.\label{eq:-6}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Finally,
 for all other possible posterior co-ordinates on the belief grid,
 the transition probability is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(i^{\prime}|i,g)=0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Maintenance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals then must expend reserve energy to maintain their health over the course of foraging,
 generating posterior energy reserves
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c(s,e^{\prime},g)=e^{\prime}+g-(s^{\prime}+e^{\prime})^{3}\cdot c_{max}\label{eq:-22}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is the posterior energy reserves after development defined as and where we assume maintenance costs scale by the volume of the individual,
 given by their total length cubed 
\begin_inset Formula $(s+e^{\prime})^{3}$
\end_inset

,
 and where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{max}$
\end_inset

 is the maintenance cost of per unit of volume of the organism.
 This then needs to be interpolated on the grid,
 giving 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e_{1}=\lfloor min(E,max(0,c(s^{\prime},e^{\prime},g)))\rfloor
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{2}=max(0,c(s^{\prime},e^{\prime},g))-e_{1}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e_{2}=min(E,(e_{1}+1))
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{1}=1-p_{2}.
\]

\end_inset

which gives a transition probability matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{c}(e^{\prime\prime}=e_{1}|s^{\prime},e^{\prime},g)=p_{1}
\]

\end_inset

and if 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}<1$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{c}(e^{\prime\prime}=e_{2}|s^{\prime},e^{\prime},g)=p_{2}
\]

\end_inset

with probability of survival of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{c}(survival|s^{\prime},e^{\prime\prime})=(1-\frac{E-e^{\prime\prime}}{E}\cdot\mu_{e})\cdot(1-\mu_{base})\cdot
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{base}$
\end_inset

 is the baseline probability of death between growth cycles due to auxiliary exogenous factors and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{e}$
\end_inset

 is the maximum probability of death given a lack of energy reserves.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Demographic stochasticity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every patch undergoes stochastic perturbations in population density,
 influenced by patch population density (e.g.
 the local extinction of a prey species).
 We assume that the probability of death is equal for all individuals so there is no change in the distribution of individual states within patches as a result of these perturbations.
 We define the resulting transition probabilities as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{stoch}(q_{0}|q_{0})=1-\gamma_{low}\label{eq:-12}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{stoch}(q_{1}|q_{0})=\gamma_{low}\label{eq:-13}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{stoch}(q_{0}|q_{1})=\gamma_{high}\label{eq:-18}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{stoch}(q_{1}|q_{1})=1-\gamma_{high}\label{eq:-19}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{low}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{high}$
\end_inset

 give the rates of transition up from low patch density states and down from high density states respectively.
 We assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{high}>\gamma_{low}$
\end_inset

,
 as high density patches are more susceptible to famine and disease outbreaks.
 
\end_layout

